;; talk/institution--replies.scm — the author of surfaces/institution.scm answers every objection to it.

;; ── canon ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-institution-canon-1 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-canon-1 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'rejected-ideas-need-new-evidence "Right that the question belongs to the maintainer and was sitting inside a decision whose decider is the user. It now has its own decision, d-institution-angle, decided by the maintainer, and d-institution-who depends on it. I do not accept that the surface is the rejected idea in full: the rejected line is about the angle used to sell xln, and a board that votes before money moves is how the protocol already works for any entity with several signers. The new evidence is written into the decision: the user named an institution among the surfaces on 2026-11-18 (said several-surfaces). Whether that is enough is the maintainer's to say."))

(reply 'r-institution-canon-2 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-canon-2 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/protocol/dispute/lending-covenant.ts" "The late window does not apply here: the rules refuse a late window on savings, so showing it would show a zero that means nothing. The missing recovery is real and is now written in. Leaving the account brings savings due at once, principal plus interest earned so far, fixed when the dispute starts. The spare-cash screen now asks what comes back if the hub never pays, shows our and the hub's locked shares beside the savings, and offers leaving; the savings flow ends by saying what leaving would return for certain and what could be lost. How savings count against what we could lose is a new maintainer decision, d-institution-savings-exposure, because I could not trace through the contract whether that claim is paid from locked collateral."))

(reply 'r-institution-canon-3 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-canon-3 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'launch-flows "Accepted that the file named no carrier and no chain. d-institution-refuses now says it in its reasons: paying, receiving, credit, savings and leaving on both launch chains, with each chain's limits; same-chain swap carried by the trader surface and the private surface's currency change; cross-chain swap by the trader surface; borrowing by the borrowing app. Whether the institution surface may leave the swaps out is already the maintainer's, since that is its decider. Rejected that this file should name owning tests: tests come with the build, every flow here carries that duty under launch-flows-need-owning-tests, and naming tests that do not exist yet would make the spec read as if they did."))

(reply 'r-institution-canon-4 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-canon-4 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'one-path-no-parallel "Right that the need asked for a store beside the runtime, and right that the screen and flow read as if the record existed. The leaning of d-institution-record is now that the runtime itself keeps the organisation's own signed frames, with the requests and votes in them, for as long as the organisation chooses, and the shared base only reads and exports them. That is one path, not two. The record screen and the audit flow now say they are not offered until the runtime keeps it (no-stubs-in-flows). Where the record lives was always the maintainer's; it still is."))

(reply 'r-institution-canon-5 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-canon-5 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/account/lifecycle/open-account.ts" "Right that a sheet voted once and kept by the wallet between votes would be a second configuration path, and nothing in the runtime holds it. Wrong that there must be an interval with unsafe values live: an opening takes response times, listing, credit and the collateral setting for one token in the same step, and a board's voted actions run in order in one frame, so as I read it the opening and the settings for the other tokens are one vote. The leaning is now prefilled-opening: each opening request carries every setting, prefilled from the last opening the board voted, which the runtime already holds. The runtime-held sheet stays as an option for the maintainer, named as new runtime work."))

(reply 'r-institution-canon-6 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-canon-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (canon 'htlc-constants-are-simnet "The default chose a number nobody has decided and called the risk nobody's. There is now a decision, d-institution-delivery, with the maintainer as decider, and the default says that on an EVM mainnet no payment through hubs with real money is offered until the deadlines are decided, and that the risk is the holder's. Working through it showed a second problem: paying another organisation in the short mode fails while its machines are off, so the leaning is by recipient."))

;; ── person ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-institution-person-1 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-person-1 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "jurisdictions/contracts/EntityProvider.sol" "Right on both counts: moving to a new organisation is the largest job it would ever do, and the margin is spent by the first loss. The previous leaning also missed a path. The chain lets a board propose its own successor with one authorisation from the current board, taking effect after the control delay. The runtime, as I read it, only submits the shareholders' form. d-institution-signer-change now leans to the board replacing itself, decided by the maintainer, who must also rule that this is not company machinery. The gap now says what the job waits on, and the setup says plainly that replacing people is not yet offered."))

(reply 'r-institution-person-2 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-person-2 :stance 'amend
  :because (guess "Accepted that telling payers to use the day-long mode makes the finance lead explain delivery to customers; that remedy is removed, and the person's list now says they cannot be asked to do it. Accepted that the cost of a closed weekend is real and must be said, not hidden: the receiving flow now ends with the hours in which payments went back to the payer. Not accepted that the surface can solve this: receiving needs enough of the board's machines to sign, and only the protocol can change that, so d-institution-reach stays with the maintainer and its options now say plainly what each one costs."))

(reply 'r-institution-person-3 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-person-3 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/entity/tx/processing/proposals.ts" "The slot is per member, not per organisation, so the finance lead's run blocks only the finance lead; only the case where every member has a request waiting is stuck. That case is real, and the decision hid it in an option. There is now d-institution-urgent-slot, leaning to one member who agrees never to put routine requests, so an urgent one can always go at once; asking the maintainer to let an author withdraw their own request is an option there. I also checked how many votes leaving takes: as I read it one, since putting it on the chain and finishing are steps the runtime sends to itself."))

(reply 'r-institution-person-4 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-person-4 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "The first screen showed deadlines and not the waiting requests it claimed to be about, and its question was two questions. It now asks one: what is waiting for one of us to decide today. It offers the vote first and shows only things a vote can still change. The figure for a waiting request does not exist in the ground, which holds only account figures; the board state comes through the board capability, and the missing figure is left for the kernel's author rather than invented here."))

(reply 'r-institution-person-5 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-person-5 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "A cap with no margin turns every fee rise during a weekend of voting into a second round of approvals, and colleagues who see the same run twice stop reading it. The other objection on this default shows it is worse than stated: one stale payment stops the whole run. There is now d-institution-fee-margin, leaning to a margin the board votes once, and voters see the most each payment may cost, not the quote. How often quotes go stale before a vote ends is the observation that would settle it, so its decider is person-evidence."))

(reply 'r-institution-person-6 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-person-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "Refusing sends away the organisation most likely to arrive first and makes it add a director only to satisfy the screen, while it still needs the runs, the position and the record. What it must not get is a false promise that someone else checked each payment. The leaning of d-institution-board-path is now allow-and-say: every screen says who can act alone, and the record marks what ran without a second person. The change-condition says what would bring refusal back."))

;; ── protocol ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-institution-protocol-1 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-protocol-1 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/watchtower/last-resort-appointment.ts" "Confirmed: the authorisation is built with one key's signature, and I found no path that builds it for a board. The decision had treated the watching service as the settled part; it is not. d-institution-reach now says that as I read it nothing answers for the organisation while its people are away, leans to asking the maintainer to let a board appoint the service, and keeps office-hours as the honest option that leaves a weekend unanswered. Every account is shown as not covered, and the long response time is now its own decision because it is the only protection left."))

(reply 'r-institution-protocol-2 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-protocol-2 :stance 'amend
  :because (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "Right that a secured total with no word on coverage is a safety claim the protocol does not back at that moment. The holders decision now says secured holds only while the board can answer in time, the coverage need asks the shared base to say not covered wherever nothing answers, and the position flow ends with that statement. The ground has no figure for coverage, so no screen can list one; this is a gap in the ground for the kernel's author, not something this surface may invent."))

(reply 'r-institution-protocol-3 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-protocol-3 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/entity/consensus/input/admission.ts" "The arithmetic is right and the default hid it. One correction: the runtime logs the low-quorum warning when the entity starts; it does not refuse the board. That makes it a judgement, not a wall, so there is now d-institution-board-size with the maintainer as decider: a margin first, a safe threshold first, or four seats with three needed, which keeps both. The default now proposes four seats and, for a board of three, says which of the two it gives up."))

(reply 'r-institution-protocol-4 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-protocol-4 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/entity/auth/authorization.ts" "Right: starting to leave is a collective action, so an organisation that has lost more members than its margin cannot even begin to leave, and moving to a new organisation is available only while it can still agree. The signer-change decision now says so, and treats the move as no remedy once agreement is lost. That is part of why the leaning moved to the board replacing itself while it still can, and why the setup proposes four seats."))

(reply 'r-institution-protocol-5 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-protocol-5 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/entity/consensus/frame/application.ts" "As I read it too: the voted actions run in order in the frame that carries the deciding vote, a refused one throws out the whole input, and the comment there says the outer signed command is evicted whole. The request keeps waiting with its earlier votes, and its author cannot put another. The batching decision now says a run is all or nothing at the last vote, leans to runs with a size limit, and the pay flow no longer promises an outcome per payment at that moment, only after the run has left. The kernel's batch capability promises an answer per action; a board's run cannot give one at admission, and that conflict is left for the kernel's author."))

(reply 'r-institution-protocol-6 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-protocol-6 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/account/config/dispute-config.ts" "The window is the two response times added, so three days for us lengthens our own exits too, and the label hid that the holder pays. The default is now labelled holder, states about seventy-three hours with a hub, and the leaving decision uses both figures instead of twenty-five hours alone. The response time is now its own decision, d-institution-response-time."))

;; ── structure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(reply 'r-institution-structure-1 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-structure-1 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/entity/auth/authorization.ts" "Right that the carve-out was a fourth option chosen inside the reasons; it is now an option of its own, voted-rules-receiving-free, beside a strict version that holds receiving for a vote. Partly wrong on the risk: what reaches us from an account is protocol work that is never put to a vote, and a claim drawn on credit we granted can raise what is owed to us only up to the line the board voted (law balance-bound), so that risk was voted with the line. It is still risk, so the claim default is now labelled holder, not nobody."))

(reply 'r-institution-structure-2 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-structure-2 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "One node lengthened the exit window that another was spending its argument shortening, and the label hid who pays. The response time now has its own decision with three options and a person-evidence decider, the default is labelled holder, and the leaving decision depends on it and quotes both window lengths."))

(reply 'r-institution-structure-3 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-structure-3 :stance 'accept
  :because (guess "The decision carrying the most risky defaults had no place where anyone made it. There is now a job to start working with a new counterparty, an opening screen, and an opening flow that signs the opening and the collateral settings in one voted request, using the kernel's effect, question, terms and standing-rules capabilities. The settlement that takes from us now has a flow too, offered from the first screen and the counterparty screen. The setup decision now leans away from a sheet kept beside the runtime."))

(reply 'r-institution-structure-4 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-structure-4 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "jurisdictions/contracts/EntityProvider.sol" "Right that the margin answers absence, not a former colleague who still holds a key and a vote, and that the options left only one inside the surface's power. The added option, a board replacing itself through the lane the chain already has, answers the job itself: the remaining members vote the departed one out as long as they reach the threshold without them. The move to a new organisation is now described in the decision for what it is, and the gap says what the job waits on."))

(reply 'r-institution-structure-5 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-structure-5 :stance 'accept
  :because (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/htlc/resolve.ts" "Payments in flight cannot be recalled before their deadline, so they do not belong on a screen about what needs us; they move to the run. A fee notice stays because the board can still leave before the rise, and a leaving deadline stays only with who started it, since a dispute started against us must be answered. The question is now one question, and the waiting requests come through the board capability, since the ground has no figure for them."))

(reply 'r-institution-structure-6 :by "opus-institution" :to 'o-institution-structure-6 :stance 'amend
  :because (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/dispute/index.ts" "Right that 'prepared' was decided silently; it now means filled in on the member's device and never put by the surface, since putting it would spend a member's only slot without asking. Right that the surface set no threshold; a default now says four working hours. Not right that leaving takes three votes: in this file the voted step records the intent and puts the dispute on the chain itself once its evidence is stable, and the runtime's scheduler finishes it after the window with no one asked (core/entity/scheduler/due-hooks.ts). The leave flow now says one vote; the case where evidence is not yet stable at the vote I have not followed, and the decision marks it as a guess. The slot problem is the new decision d-institution-urgent-slot."))
